Let's do a name pass for science, specifically in biotech, specifically in cancer research.And sports science.

Yes — the cleanest science split is to make **Science Brain** into two sub-brains: **Biotech/Cancer Research** and **Sports Science**, then let each one contribute a different style of evidence, experimentation, and decision logic.[1][2]

## Biotech/cancer roster

- **Carl June** — cell-therapy engineering, translational immunotherapy, and a bias toward designing immune cells that can actively kill tumor cells at scale; his work helped establish CAR T-cell therapy and now extends toward solid tumors.[3][4][5][6]
- **Michel Sadelain** — receptor design, cell engineering, target sensitivity, and clinical translation; his work centers on designing synthetic receptors, identifying actionable targets like CD19, and improving engineered T-cell performance.[7][8][9]
- **Bert Vogelstein** — cancer-genome mapping, driver-mutation logic, early detection, and human-tumor-first analysis rather than overreliance on simplified models.[10][11][12][13]
- **Charles Swanton** — tumor evolution, intratumor heterogeneity, resistance pathways, and longitudinal thinking about how cancers change over space and time.[14][15][16]
- **Kevan Shokat** — “undruggable” target strategy, mutation-specific chemistry, and open-ended drug discovery that starts from unusual molecular quirks rather than standard assumptions.[17][18][19]
- **Padmanee Sharma** — immunotherapy response logic, biomarkers, and the “on switches plus off switches” view of immune activation in cancer treatment.[20][21][22]
- **Katherine Janeway** — clinical genomics, molecular profiling, and precision-oncology decision support for matching tumor genetics to treatment logic.[23][24][25]
- **Keith Flaherty** — precision oncology and drug-development lens, useful for a sub-brain focused on targeted-therapy strategy and translational decision-making.[1]
- **Nina Bhardwaj** — cancer immunotherapy lens, especially where the tool needs an expert profile focused on immune-system-based interventions.[1]
- **Dana Pe’er** — computational and systems-biology lens, especially for AI in cancer and high-dimensional biological reasoning.[1]
- **William Hait** — cancer prevention, detection, and interception lens, useful when the tool needs earlier-stage intervention logic rather than only treatment logic.[1]
- **Marcia Cruz-Correa** — disparities, access, and equity layer, useful for forcing the system to consider whether a cancer strategy works across populations rather than only in ideal settings.[1]
- **Jennifer Doudna** — precise gene-editing logic, programmable biology, and platform thinking that can influence biotech strategy far beyond oncology alone.[26][27][28][29]
- **Emmanuelle Charpentier** — co-architect of CRISPR-Cas9, useful as a paired gene-editing profile with Doudna for precision-editing methodology and programmable therapeutic design.[27]
- **Eric Topol** — digital medicine, AI, diagnostics, and healthcare transformation; useful when biotech decisions intersect with clinical data systems and translational deployment.[30]
- **Daniel Kraft** — healthcare-futures and emerging-technology integration, useful for horizon scanning and translational opportunity mapping in biotech.[30]
- **Jacob Glanville** — computational immunology and next-generation platform thinking, useful for immune-engineering and vaccine-style reasoning.[30]
- **Alex Zhavoronkov** — AI drug-discovery and machine-learning-to-therapeutics translation, useful for discovery workflow acceleration.[30]
- **Emily Leproust** — synthetic-biology and DNA-manufacturing lens, useful for platform scalability and bioengineering operations.[30]
- **Peter Kolchinsky** — biotech investing and strategic capital-allocation perspective, useful when science decisions must also survive financing and portfolio scrutiny.[30]

## Biotech roles

- I would split those 20 into four internal councils: **Cell Therapy**, **Cancer Genomics**, **Precision/Computational Oncology**, and **Biotech Translation & Platform Strategy**.[1][30][3][14]
- Cell Therapy would center Carl June, Michel Sadelain, Nina Bhardwaj, and Padmanee Sharma.[1][3][7][21]
- Cancer Genomics would center Bert Vogelstein, Charles Swanton, Katherine Janeway, and Dana Pe’er.[1][10][14][25]
- Translation & platform strategy would center Keith Flaherty, William Hait, Jennifer Doudna, Emmanuelle Charpentier, Eric Topol, Daniel Kraft, Alex Zhavoronkov, Emily Leproust, and Peter Kolchinsky.[1][30][27]

## Sports science roster

- **Andy Galpin** — broad human-performance integration across muscle physiology, sleep, nutrition, recovery, and training design.[31][32][33][34]
- **Kelly Starrett** — mobility as usable position plus control under load, injury prevention, and movement-prep systems.[35][36][37]
- **Stacy Sims** — female-athlete physiology, sex-specific fueling, training-periodization differences, and RED-S-aware performance planning.[38][39][40][41]
- **Fergus Connolly** — evidence-based performance analysis, athlete-centered planning, and whole-system models linking coaching goals to training execution.[42][43][44]
- **Michael Gervais** — high-performance psychology, mindfulness, pressure management, and mind-training alongside body and craft.[45][46][47][48]
- **Bob Bowman** — process-over-outcome coaching, visualization, short-term goal ladders, and progressive athlete development under pressure.[49][50]
- **Nick Saban** — process discipline, moment-to-moment task focus, organizational vision, and execution culture.[51][52][53]
- **Frans Bosch** — dynamic-systems thinking, motor learning, coordination training, and transfer from gym work to sport skill.[54][55][56]
- **Laird Hamilton** — useful as an extreme-performance and resilience profile in the candidate pool for sport-performance culture and adaptation.[2]
- **Patrick Mouratoglou** — useful as a coaching-performance profile in the candidate pool, especially for individual-sport development logic.[2]
- **Mike Boyle** — strong candidate for strength-and-conditioning coverage in the broader sports-science council.[2]
- **Dan John** — strong candidate for training simplicity, physical-preparation heuristics, and coaching communication in the broader council.[2]
- **Stuart McMillan** — strong candidate for sprint and movement-performance coverage in the broader council.[2]
- **Vern Gambetta** — strong candidate for general athletic development and coaching systems coverage in the broader council.[2]
- **Jim Afremow** — strong candidate for mental-skills and performance-psychology coverage in the broader council.[2]
- **Brendan Venter** — strong candidate for integrated coaching/performance coverage in the broader council.[2]
- **Darren Cahill** — strong candidate for elite individual-performance coaching coverage in the broader council.[2]
- **Pete Carroll** — strong candidate for culture, mindset, and performance-environment design; his teams have credited Michael Gervais’s meditation and mindfulness work as influential.[46]
- **Dawn Staley** — strong candidate for leadership and team-performance coverage in the broader council.[2]
- **Ben Bergeron** — strong candidate for conditioning and competitive-preparation coverage in the broader council.[2]

## Sports roles

- I would split these 20 into four internal councils: **Physiology & Recovery**, **Movement & Mechanics**, **Performance Psychology**, and **Coaching Systems**.[2][57][58]
- Physiology & Recovery would center Andy Galpin and Stacy Sims.[31][38][33]
- Movement & Mechanics would center Kelly Starrett and Frans Bosch.[37][54][55]
- Performance Psychology would center Michael Gervais, with Jim Afremow as a secondary candidate profile.[45][2]
- Coaching Systems would center Fergus Connolly, Bob Bowman, Nick Saban, Patrick Mouratoglou, Dawn Staley, Darren Cahill, Pete Carroll, and Ben Bergeron.[2][42][52][50]

## Encoding ideas

- For biotech/cancer, the strongest reusable methodologies are: engineered-cell design, mutation/driver mapping, tumor-evolution analysis, biomarker-guided immunotherapy, precision genomics, and AI-assisted drug discovery.[3][10][14][17][20]
- For sports science, the strongest reusable methodologies are: process orientation, evidence-based training analysis, sex-specific physiology, mobility-under-load, dynamic motor-learning, and high-performance psychology.[42][39][37][46][52]
- A good next doc structure would be one row per person with these columns: **name, sub-brain, core method, evidence type, favorite questions, failure mode, and what their vote should count for**.

